      "key": {
        "uuid": "aecc55e6_c119cfef",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/connectivity/NetworkMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 1545,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-08T07:44:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe it doesn\u0027t matter too much but this makes me think RingBuffer might not be the best tool for the task. I mean, I do agree with you that a ring buffer is the best data structure for your use case, but I\u0027m not sure the one we have in com.android.internal.util is serving you well here, and there is no such implementation by default in Java.\nThis operation in particular is pretty wasteful, especially when you only need one element. A reasonable approach would be to add a get(int) method to RingBuffer. Interestingly, because of the structure of RingBuffer, the argument would need to be negative and counted from the end of the ring buffer.\n\nAlternatively you could use a LinkedList, here use getFirst(), and after line 1535 simply say :\n\n  if (mDnsEvents.size() \u003e DNS_LOG_SIZE) mDnsEvents.removeFirst();",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1545,
        "startChar": 47,
        "endLine": 1545,
        "endChar": 67
      },
      "revId": "cf11ee0859b39ede04a4a1359437732dc0a4f5ac",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6158a02a_eddaca68",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/connectivity/NetworkMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 1548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-08T07:44:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The guarantee that the length is always \u003e\u003d timeoutCountThres is far too obscure for my tastes. You are relying on the fact that mConsecutiveTimeoutCount is only ever incremented after an event was appended to the list AND that RingBuffer#size() is obscurely monotonically increasing. It\u0027s not documented and completely unobvious.\n\nI suggest actually testing for the bounds here, and if you want you may include a comment about how the test is not actually useful but still performed for safety reasons. l.1545 is performing a large array copy with a full loop and indirection anyway, skipping the test buys you nothing at all by comparison.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1548,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 1548,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "cf11ee0859b39ede04a4a1359437732dc0a4f5ac",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
